exp="A grey-white ring at the edge of the cornea is <b>corneal arcus</b> (lipid deposition). In older adults it is common and of little meaning (arcus senilis), but <b>before age 45 it is highly specific for familial hypercholesterolemia</b> (as are tendon xanthomas). It should prompt a <b>lipid panel</b>, a familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) clinical score (such as Dutch Lipid Clinic criteria), and cascade screening of relatives; measure lipoprotein(a) once. Xanthelasma, in contrast, has low specificity.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Corneal arcus before 45 suggests familial hypercholesterolemia — check lipids and screen relatives.",
